The design and use of state estimation techniques is fundamental in a plethora of applications, such as robotics, tracking, guidance and navigation systems \[[@B1-sensors-21-02086],[@B2-sensors-21-02086],[@B3-sensors-21-02086],[@B4-sensors-21-02086]\]. For a linear dynamic system, the Kalman filter (KF) is the the best linear minimum mean square error (MSE) estimator. The most widespread solution for nonlinear systems is to resort to system linearisations, leading to the so-called linearised or extended KF (EKF) \[[@B3-sensors-21-02086]\]. In both cases, as well as for more advanced techniques such as sigma-point filters \[[@B5-sensors-21-02086]\], the main assumption is perfect system knowledge: (i) Known process and measurement functions, and their parameters, (ii) known inputs, and (iii) known noise statistics (i.e., first and second order moments for the KF and EKF). However, these are rather strong assumptions in real-life applications, where the noise statistics' parameters may be unknown to a certain extent, inputs may be uncertain and system parameters may be misspecified. The performance degradation of minimum MSE estimators under model mismatch has been widely reported in the literature \[[@B6-sensors-21-02086],[@B7-sensors-21-02086],[@B8-sensors-21-02086],[@B9-sensors-21-02086],[@B10-sensors-21-02086]\], which is a reason why there exists a need to develop robust filtering techniques able to cope with mismatched systems.
Notice that in the robust filtering literature, a lot of effort has been devoted to the mitigation of possible noise statistics' parameters misspecification, that is, to coping with non-nominal or unexpected noise behaviours. In that perspective, we may cite the robust KFs able to counteract the presence of outliers \[[@B11-sensors-21-02086]\], the ones that estimate Gaussian noise covariances \[[@B12-sensors-21-02086],[@B13-sensors-21-02086]\], or alternative formulations to deal with heavy-tailed distributions \[[@B14-sensors-21-02086],[@B15-sensors-21-02086]\]. In contrast, few contributions explored how to counteract a mismatch on system matrices/functions or the filter initialisation. Within the KF framework, the latter can be solved by either the information filter form of the KF (\[[@B3-sensors-21-02086]\] §6.2) or the so-called Fisher initialisation \[[@B16-sensors-21-02086]\], which can be generalised by imposing initial distortionless constraints \[[@B17-sensors-21-02086]\], i.e., the so-called minimum variance distortionless response (MVDR) estimators. In order to further generalise these MVDR results, how to incorporate non-stationary constraints within the KF has been recently proposed by Villà-Valls et al. \[[@B18-sensors-21-02086]\], leading to a general linearly constrained KF (LCKF) formulation, which has also been shown concerning linear systems to generalise the results proposed by Teixeira et al. \[[@B19-sensors-21-02086]\]. Indeed, the use of linear constraints (LCs) has been shown to be a promising robust filter design solution in order to mitigate the impact of misspecified system matrices \[[@B18-sensors-21-02086]\], which complements the existing results cited above for noise parameter mismatch mitigation. Notice that other alternatives exist to estimate unknown system parameters/inputs, such

