c != EOF does not increment the string index of c. stdin is the input-buffer. You are reading from stdin (with getchar) and writing to stdout (with putchar). When you call getchar(), a single-character is read from stdin, and the file-position-indicator for stdin is advanced-by-one (to the next character).
When you read a character from stdin (advancing to the next character) and write one character to stdout within a loop, you consume all characters in stdin one-by-one until you reach EOF for stdin (i.e. you have read all the characters available in the input-buffer)

The behaviour that "it prints the entire string" after you hit Enter is because stdout (standard output) is buffered. If it would not be buffered, each character you type will be printed immediately (duplicating your input).


more than likely 'echo' is on, so each char is immediately shown on the terminal.  2) when an output is made, followed by an input (stdout/stdin) the output buffer is flushed before the input is performed. 3) the loop also echo's any newline char (from the user hitting the 'return/enter' key) because the call to getchar() will retrieve anything in the input stream, include the 'enter' key.   However, Note: the program sees nothing until the user presses the 'enter/return' key


@user3629249 Yes, but that's because the terminal input is also line-buffered, independent of what stdin gets.

In reality, terminal I/O is 'cooked  If you want to have the application receive the characters without the user having to press the enter key, then change the terminal I/O mode to raw.   But then the application will have to handle all control keys and editing keys and etc etc.

